# One ARP reply, 42 octets, as the Linux kernel put it on a TAP device.
#
# ⚠ Captured, not written by hand. If this file is ever regenerated, capture it
# again — a frame typed out from memory is not a fixture (`CLAUDE.md` §1).
#
# How it was captured:
#   Arch Linux, kernel 7.0.2-arch1-1, x86_64, 2026-08-27
#   unshare -Urn (no sudo), tap0 with the default MTU of 1500
#   ip addr add 10.0.0.1/24 dev tap0; ip link set tap0 up
#   a probe outside src/ attached to tap0 and wrote one ARP request:
#     ar$op = 1, ar$sha 02:00:00:00:00:02, ar$spa 10.0.0.2, ar$tpa 10.0.0.1
#   the first frame read back whose octets 12..13 were 08 06 and whose ar$op was 2
#
# ⚠ The kernel answered for its own address. So ar$sha and the ethernet source
# are whatever hardware address the kernel picked for tap0 on that run; they are
# different on the next one, which is why this file is a recording and no check
# expects them to be reproduced.
#
# ⚠ 42 octets. There is no padding to 60, and there is no FCS.
# ⚠ ar$tha is the requester's hardware address — RFC 826 calls that field
# "Hardware address of target of this packet (if known)", and here it was known.
02 00 00 00 00 02 a2 0f 1b 1e 44 4b 08 06 00 01
08 00 06 04 00 02 a2 0f 1b 1e 44 4b 0a 00 00 01
02 00 00 00 00 02 0a 00 00 02
